{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"AC3 measurement, captured once the host was idle enough to answer `du` (07:5x PDT, after the unwedge, so these are the SAME bytes that filled the 30-GiB disk — only the denominator changed).\n\n```\n/            28G\n  /var       26G\n    /var/lib          21G\n      /var/lib/docker 17G\n        overlay2      16G      <-- 67 layer directories\n        volumes      929M\n        buildkit      86M\n        image        7.7M\n    /var/tmp         3.6G\n    /var/log         1.8G      (journal 1.7G)\n    /var/cache       154M\n  /usr        1.9G\n```\n\nDocker's OWN accounting for the same tree:\n\n```\nTYPE            TOTAL  ACTIVE  SIZE      RECLAIMABLE\nImages          4      4       2.802GB   144.4MB (5%)\nContainers      4      4       4.815GB   0B (0%)\nLocal Volumes   11     1       962.2MB   1.207MB (0%)\nBuild Cache     41     0       920.4MB   920.4MB\n```\n`docker images -f dangling=true -q | wc -l` -> **0**\n\n## This is why four rounds of prune-based mitigation did not hold\n\nDocker accounts for ~9.5 GB. `overlay2` on disk is **16 GB**. About **6.5 GB is orphaned layer state that Docker does not know it owns** — 67 layer directories backing only 4 images and 4 containers, with ZERO dangling images.\n\n`docker system prune` frees only what the table calls RECLAIMABLE: **144 MB + 920 MB ~= 1.06 GB**, about 3.5% of the 29 GB working set. The pressure-triggered prune added in `sour-plugugly-rhino` and hardened in later tickets was therefore structurally incapable of recovering the disk, no matter how often or how early it fired. Every incident in this chain re-tuned the TRIGGER for a reclaim whose CEILING was ~1 GB against a 29 GB problem.\n\nAnd the root disk has **three independent accumulators**, only one of which prune touches at all:\n\n1. **orphaned `overlay2` layers** — ~6.5 GB invisible to prune (needs the daemon's own GC, or a stop-daemon + orphan sweep).\n2. **`/var/tmp` — 3.6 GB** — not Docker's at all; never touched by any prune. Consistent with `scowlful-bovine-dragonfly`'s finding that one-off investigation evidence lands on disk and is never swept.\n3. **journald — 1.7 GB** — no `SystemMaxUse` cap in effect; also never touched by prune.\n\nSo AC4's \"bounded ceiling, not faster reclaim\" should be read strictly: a fix that only makes prune more eager repeats this bug a fifth time. The ceiling has to bind all three accumulators — a journald `SystemMaxUse=`, a `/var/tmp` retention sweep, and an overlay2 reclaim that can actually reach orphaned layers.\n\nNote also that the ~9.5 GB Docker DOES account for is dominated by **4.815 GB of container writable layers with 0 B reclaimable** — long-lived containers accumulating writes inside their own layers. Restarting/recreating containers is what releases that, which is likely why a reboot has historically appeared to \"fix\" this for a while and then not."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788360590250544001,"uuid":"633ce1f6-2412-4935-9cf8-100fcc53c521"}